<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Warnier Law</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1873</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Loi Warnier</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Warnier Law of July 1873, named after the deputy Auguste Warnier, extended French property law to land in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>. By breaking up collectively held tribal and family land into individual titles that could be sold, it allowed European settlers and speculators to acquire large areas of Algerian land. Coming soon after the confiscations that followed the Mokrani revolt of 1871, it accelerated the dispossession of the rural population.</p></div>
